                        "review": "So, this is my initial thoughts of the game. I have not spent a lot of time in this game but I wanted people to understand what the game is like:\n\nCons:\nThe game seems to be stuck in the early 2000s. Graphically, some of the controls, navigaing through all the menus really seems to be something from yesteryear. For the most part this does not bother me I am just hoping though the development the graphics get a big update with more polish.\n\nThis game seems to be a bit pricey for what you are getting. Now with that being said I still am early on and it could really open up at which case I would update this statement. At the same time this is not a big corporation and they need support. I do not have a problem paying what I did. If you really love this genre of game then you will see all the pros and not have a problem with the price.\nUPDATE: The more that I have played it the more I believe the price point was at a good mark. For everything you are getting for this game the price is a good deal. The reason I did not take down the part of the fact that is seemed over-priced is that I wanted players to see is that if they think initially that it is underwelming that it gets a lot better. Especially if they plan on updating it and adding new content as well as polish.\n\n\nPros:\nThe biggest pro and the whole reason I got the game is that you can build some of the most realistic looking medieval villages/towns. This genre of game, the medieval/fantasy village/city builder, is one of my favorate genres. I have played many of these kinds of games, both finished with plenty of polish and early access with a ways to go. Almost every single one of those games the villages look too good or two clean. They look like everything was built on a grid, including farmlands. Where as this isn't a bad thing it really seems to take away from the realism of it. Archamon really does look and feel like a medieval village. It's not too clean of a layout and it really feels like a village.\n\nThe terrain. Most of the games in this genre really have a hard time with terrain. Most of these games do not not know how to handle building on terrain because everything is on a grid. Archamon building on a variety of terrians really works and adds to the realism appearance of the medieval village. \n\nThe farming lands. Going with what I initially said, most games the farm is square and follows a grid. Archamon does not, they fill in areas and if you have a building near the farm lands it plants crops around that building. Again it adds to the overall realism of the game.\n\n\nWhat I'd love to see in the future for this game is to see it really update graphically and become really polished, and then open up to the steam workshop for people to make awesome buildings, industries, soldiers, etc. This would add so much replay value to this game!\n\nOverall, this game shows a lot of promise and I really look forward to spending plenty of time with this game. I hope to see updates, especially graphically that updates the textures, but this is a solid starting game. If you are a fan of this genre then you should defintely give it a try. If you are not then I would suggest to watch videos and see if this game is for you. I'd like to say thanks to the Archamon dev team!",
